# 1. Target theorem

Let T be a tree on n vertices and

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil.
$$

The target is

$$
n_2(T)\le 2q-2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
b(T)\le q.
$$

Equivalently,

$$
\boxed{
b(T)\ge q+1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
n_2(T)\ge2q-1.
}
$$

# 2. Current candidate lemma

The previous analysis proposed:

### Critical Thread Packing Lemma

Let T be a minimum-order counterexample to the target theorem.

Thus

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil,
\qquad
b(T)\ge q+1,
$$

and T has minimum possible order among all such counterexamples.

Then there should exist a failed q-round burning configuration together with pairwise vertex-disjoint paths

$$
P_1,\ldots,P_q
$$

such that

$$
|V(P_i)\cap V_2(T)|\ge2
\qquad (1\le i\le q-1),
$$

and

$$
|V(P_q)\cap V_2(T)|\ge1.
$$

Consequently,

$$
n_2(T)
\ge
\sum_{i=1}^q
|V(P_i)\cap V_2(T)|
\ge
2(q-1)+1
=
2q-1.
$$

The counting step is trivial.

The entire mathematical problem is:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Why should such disjoint critical paths exist?}
}
$$

