# 5. Third task — exploit minimality

This is the most important part.

Assume T is a minimum-order counterexample.

Ask what minimality implies.

For every vertex v, consider deleting or contracting a suitable local structure around v.

Can minimality imply that certain structures cannot occur?

Investigate:

### Degree-2 vertex deletion

If v has degree 2, what happens after suppressing v?

Can one show that a minimum counterexample cannot contain a degree-2 vertex that is "non-critical"?

### Leaf deletion

If v is a leaf, consider T-v.

Can the burning sequence of T-v be extended to T?

If yes, what structural conditions must therefore hold around every leaf?

### Branching vertex

Can a minimum counterexample contain a branching vertex adjacent to several short branches?

If not, prove why.

The goal is to derive structural constraints from minimality rather than assuming that branching is harmless.

# 6. Fourth task — exploit the exact threshold q = ceil(sqrt n)

Do NOT treat q as an independent parameter.

Always enforce

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil.
$$

Therefore

$$
(q-1)^2<n\le q^2.
$$

Define the square deficit

$$
d=q^2-n,
\qquad
0\le d\le2q-2.
$$

Investigate whether the square deficit is structurally related to the number of degree-2 vertices.

