# 12. Tackle the burning-order issue correctly

The previous analysis made an important distinction between static ball covers and valid burning sequences.

Do NOT repeat the flawed argument that

$$
B(c,1),B(c,0)
$$

using the same center proves a difference.

That only shows that an arbitrary ball-cover certificate need not itself be a legal burning sequence.

The real question is:

> If there exist DISTINCT vertices \(v_1,\ldots,v_q\) such that
>
> $$
> V(T)\subseteq
> \bigcup_{i=1}^q B(v_i,q-i),
> $$
>
> must there always exist an ordering of these centers that is a valid burning sequence?

Investigate this carefully for trees.

If the answer is yes, prove it or identify the known structural reason.

If the answer is no, find the smallest tree counterexample.

This question matters because a successful proof may be much easier in the static ball-cover formulation.

